-- Q1: medium / L3 — Sec 2044 QTIP inclusion in surviving spouse's estate
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Robert Mercer died in 2020, and his estate elected QTIP treatment for a $5,000,000 marital trust for his wife, Laura. Laura received all income from the trust during her lifetime but had no power to direct the remainder. Laura dies in 2026 when the trust assets are worth $7,200,000. Determine the estate tax consequence to Laura's estate.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The full $7,200,000 is included in Laura's gross estate under Section 2044", "Only $5,000,000 (the original funding amount) is included in Laura's gross estate", "Nothing is included because Laura had no power of appointment over the trust", "Only the $2,200,000 of appreciation is included in Laura's gross estate"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 2044 requires that all QTIP property for which a marital deduction was previously allowed be included in the surviving spouse's gross estate at its date-of-death fair market value. The full $7,200,000 is included because the QTIP election was made on Robert's estate tax return. Wrong (B): The original funding amount is irrelevant; Section 2044 uses FMV at the surviving spouse's death. Wrong (C): A power of appointment is not required for QTIP inclusion; the prior marital deduction election triggers mandatory inclusion. Wrong (D): Section 2044 includes the entire value, not just appreciation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L2 — Sec 2040(b) spousal joint interest vs non-spouse contribution rule
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Elena Torres paid $900,000 to buy a beach condo and titled it as a qualified joint interest with her husband, Marco, who contributed nothing. Elena dies in 2026 when the condo is worth $1,400,000. How does Section 2040(b) determine the amount included in Elena's gross estate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$1,400,000, because Elena funded the entire purchase", "$700,000, because spousal joint interests are automatically included at 50% of fair market value regardless of contribution", "$900,000, limited to Elena's original contribution", "Nothing, because the marital deduction offsets the inclusion"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 2040(b) provides a special rule for qualified joint interests between spouses: exactly one-half of the property's date-of-death FMV is included in the decedent's gross estate, regardless of which spouse provided the consideration. The inclusion is $1,400,000 x 50% = $700,000. Wrong (A): The full-contribution rule under Section 2040(a) applies only to non-spouse co-owners. Wrong (C): Original contribution is irrelevant under the spousal rule of Section 2040(b). Wrong (D): The marital deduction is a separate computation that reduces the taxable estate; it does not eliminate gross estate inclusion.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Gift of encumbered property (net gift value)
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Diane Patterson gifts rental property with a fair market value of $600,000 and an outstanding mortgage of $200,000 to her son, who assumes the mortgage as a condition of the gift. The annual exclusion is $19,000. Determine the value of the gift for gift tax purposes.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$600,000, the full fair market value of the property", "$581,000, the fair market value less the annual exclusion", "$400,000, the fair market value less the mortgage assumed by the donee", "$381,000, the fair market value less the mortgage and the annual exclusion"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 2512, when a donee assumes a liability on gifted property, the amount of the gift equals the FMV minus the liability assumed. The donee's assumption of the $200,000 mortgage is treated as consideration, reducing the gift value to $600,000 - $200,000 = $400,000. The annual exclusion is then applied separately to determine the taxable gift. Wrong (A): This ignores the consideration provided by the donee's assumption of the mortgage. Wrong (B): This subtracts the annual exclusion but ignores the mortgage assumption. Wrong (D): This combines the gift value and taxable gift calculations into a single step; the question asks for the value of the gift, not the taxable gift.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Form 709 filing requirement triggers
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Under what circumstance must a donor file Form 709, United States Gift Tax Return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["When the donor makes any gift of a present interest during the year", "When the donor makes gifts to a qualified charity exceeding $19,000", "When the donor receives a gift exceeding $19,000 from another individual", "When the donor makes a gift to any single donee exceeding the annual exclusion amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): A donor must file Form 709 when a gift to any single donee exceeds the annual exclusion amount ($19,000 for 2026). Gifts within the exclusion require no filing. The return is also required for gifts of future interests regardless of amount and when gift splitting is elected. Wrong (A): Gifts of present interests at or below the annual exclusion do not require filing. Wrong (B): Charitable gifts are deductible and generally do not require Form 709 unless they involve split-interest trusts. Wrong (C): The donee never files Form 709; the gift tax is imposed on the donor.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Sec 2035(a) scope — most gifts not pulled back
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Warren Ellsworth made an outright gift of $500,000 in publicly traded stock to his daughter in 2024. Warren dies in 2026. His estate attorney must determine whether the gifted stock is included in Warren's gross estate under Section 2035. How should the stock be treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The stock is not included in the gross estate because Section 2035(a) only applies to transfers of life insurance and certain retained-interest property, not outright gifts of stock", "The stock is included at its $500,000 gift-date value because the transfer occurred within three years of death", "The stock is included at its date-of-death fair market value under the general three-year rule", "The stock is excluded only if the gift was reported on Form 709 and gift tax was paid"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 2035(a) pulls transfers back into the gross estate only when they involve life insurance policies (Section 2042) or interests that would have been included under Sections 2036-2038 or 2042. Outright gifts of stock are not subject to the three-year inclusion rule. Wrong (B): The general three-year pullback does not apply to outright gifts of property. Wrong (C): Same error; outright stock gifts are outside the scope of Section 2035(a). Wrong (D): Filing Form 709 and paying gift tax have no bearing on whether Section 2035(a) applies.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Reverse QTIP election for GST purposes
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Daniel Kensington's estate creates a QTIP trust for his surviving wife, allocating the marital deduction. Daniel's executor also wants to allocate Daniel's GST exemption to the trust so that distributions to grandchildren after the wife's death are exempt from GSTT. Under normal QTIP rules, the surviving spouse is treated as the transferor for GST purposes, which would waste Daniel's GST exemption. Assess the planning strategy the executor should use.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Elect portability of Daniel's unused GST exemption to his wife", "Make a reverse QTIP election under Section 2652(a)(3) so that Daniel remains the transferor for GST purposes", "Disclaim the QTIP election and fund a bypass trust instead", "Allocate the GST exemption directly to the trust beneficiaries rather than the trust"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 2652(a)(3) allows the executor to elect to treat the QTIP trust as if the QTIP election had not been made solely for GST purposes. This reverse QTIP election keeps the decedent as the transferor for GST purposes, allowing the decedent's GST exemption to be allocated to the trust. Wrong (A): GST exemption is not portable between spouses; portability applies only to the estate/gift tax exemption. Wrong (C): Disclaiming the QTIP election would forfeit the marital deduction, creating unnecessary estate tax. Wrong (D): GST exemption is allocated to trusts or transfers, not directly to beneficiaries.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Gift valuation date (FMV at date of transfer)
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Rachel Whitmore transfers 5,000 shares of publicly traded stock to her nephew on June 1, 2026, when the shares trade at $40 per share (total FMV of $200,000). The nephew acknowledges receipt on June 15, when the shares trade at $52 per share. By December 31, 2026, the shares trade at $60 per share. What value is used to calculate the taxable gift?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$60 per share ($300,000), the value on the last day of the tax year", "The average of the June 1 and June 15 values", "$200,000, the fair market value on the date of the transfer", "$260,000, the fair market value on the date the donee acknowledged receipt"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 2512 and Treasury Regulation 25.2512-1, the value of a gift is determined as of the date the transfer is complete. For publicly traded stock, this is the date the donor relinquishes dominion and control. The gift is valued at $200,000 (5,000 shares x $40). Wrong (A): Year-end value is irrelevant to gift valuation. Wrong (B): Averaging is used for publicly traded stock within a single trading day (high/low), not between two dates. Wrong (D): The donee's acknowledgment date does not determine when the gift is complete.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Adequate disclosure on Form 709 and statute of limitations
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$In 2020, Gloria Remington transferred a 25% interest in her family LLC to her son and reported the gift on Form 709 with a value of $800,000 based on a qualified appraisal. The return included a complete description of the methodology, a copy of the appraisal, and all information required by Treasury Regulation 301.6501(c)-1(f). The IRS did not audit the return. In 2026, Gloria dies, and the IRS seeks to revalue the 2020 gift at $1,400,000 to increase the estate tax base. Assess whether the IRS can adjust the value.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The IRS can adjust the value because gift tax returns are always open for revaluation at death", "The IRS can adjust the value because the gift involved a valuation discount subject to special scrutiny", "The IRS can adjust the value only if it can prove fraud or intentional undervaluation", "The IRS cannot adjust the value because adequate disclosure started the three-year statute of limitations, which has expired"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 6501(c)(9) and Treasury Regulation 301.6501(c)-1(f), when a gift is adequately disclosed on a timely filed Form 709, the statute of limitations for that gift is three years from the filing date. Once expired, the IRS cannot revalue the gift for gift tax or estate tax purposes. Gloria's 2020 gift is closed. Wrong (A): Adequate disclosure prevents unlimited revaluation; without it the statute remains open indefinitely. Wrong (B): Valuation discounts do not override the statute of limitations when adequate disclosure requirements are met. Wrong (C): The fraud exception requires clear and convincing evidence of fraud, not mere disagreement on value; the question states proper disclosure was made.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: easy / L2 — Sec 7872 de minimis exception (loans ≤ $10,000)
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Robert Langford lends $8,000 to his adult daughter at 0% interest. The applicable federal rate is 4.5%. The loan is not used to purchase income-producing assets. Under Section 7872, how is the below-market loan treated for gift tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gift is imputed because the de minimis exception exempts gift loans of $10,000 or less from Section 7872", "A gift of $360 (the forgone interest at 4.5%) is imputed to Robert", "The entire $8,000 loan amount is treated as a taxable gift", "A gift is imputed only if Robert does not charge at least 2% interest"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 7872(c)(2) provides a de minimis exception for gift loans with an aggregate outstanding balance of $10,000 or less between the lender and borrower. Because the loan is $8,000, no interest is imputed and no gift results. This exception does not apply if the loan proceeds are used to purchase income-producing assets. Wrong (B): This applies the general imputed interest rule but ignores the de minimis exception. Wrong (C): A loan is not a gift of the principal amount; only the forgone interest could be a gift. Wrong (D): The 2% threshold is not part of the below-market loan rules.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Unified transfer tax system (shared rate schedule)
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$What is the relationship between the federal gift tax and the federal estate tax?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["They are separate taxes with independent rate schedules and separate exemptions", "They share a unified rate schedule and a single cumulative lifetime exemption", "The gift tax applies only to transfers exceeding $1,000,000, while the estate tax has no threshold", "The estate tax replaced the gift tax for transfers after 2010"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sections 2001 and 2502, the gift tax and estate tax use the same unified rate schedule (with a top rate of 40%) and share a single cumulative lifetime exemption (~$14.50 million for 2026). Taxable gifts during life reduce the exemption available at death. Wrong (A): The taxes are unified, not independent; they share both the rate schedule and the exemption. Wrong (C): There is no separate $1,000,000 gift tax threshold; the annual exclusion and lifetime exemption apply. Wrong (D): Both taxes remain in effect; the gift tax was not replaced.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Sec 2032A maximum reduction cap
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Douglas Whitaker dies in 2026 owning farmland with a fair market value of $5,000,000 based on highest and best use (development) and $1,800,000 based on qualified agricultural use. The estate qualifies for the Section 2032A special use valuation election. The executor elects special use valuation. What value is used for the farmland in the gross estate?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$1,800,000, the full qualified use value", "$5,000,000, because the reduction exceeds the statutory maximum", "$3,610,000, because the maximum reduction under Section 2032A is capped at $1,390,000 for 2026", "$2,500,000, representing a 50% reduction from the highest and best use value"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 2032A limits the aggregate reduction in value to $1,390,000 (2026, indexed for inflation). The difference between FMV and qualified use value is $5,000,000 - $1,800,000 = $3,200,000, which exceeds the cap. Therefore, the estate can only reduce the value by $1,390,000, resulting in an estate inclusion of $5,000,000 - $1,390,000 = $3,610,000. Wrong (A): The full qualified use value would require a $3,200,000 reduction, exceeding the statutory cap. Wrong (B): No reduction would waste the available election. Wrong (D): There is no 50% reduction rule under Section 2032A.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Transferee (donee) liability for unpaid gift tax (Sec 6324(b))
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Calvin Mitchell makes a $16,000,000 taxable gift to his niece in 2026. The gift tax liability exceeds Calvin's remaining unified credit, resulting in $600,000 in gift tax due. Calvin fails to pay the tax and becomes insolvent. The IRS seeks to collect the unpaid gift tax. Who bears liability for the unpaid tax?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only Calvin, because the donor is exclusively liable for gift tax under all circumstances", "The IRS, because it failed to collect from Calvin before he became insolvent", "Calvin's estate, but only if he dies within three years of the gift", "The niece, as transferee, is personally liable for the unpaid gift tax up to the value of the gift received"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 6324(b), if the donor fails to pay the gift tax, the donee becomes personally liable for the tax to the extent of the value of the gift. This transferee liability ensures the IRS can collect from the recipient when the donor cannot pay. Wrong (A): While the donor is primarily liable, Section 6324(b) creates secondary liability for the donee. Wrong (B): The IRS does not absorb tax liabilities due to donor insolvency. Wrong (C): The three-year rule under Section 2035 relates to estate inclusion, not gift tax collection.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Exercise of general power of appointment as taxable transfer (Sec 2514)
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Margaret Holloway holds a general power of appointment over a $3,000,000 trust established by her late father. In 2026, Margaret exercises the power by directing the trustee to distribute the entire $3,000,000 to her two adult children in equal shares. Margaret has already used $12,000,000 of her lifetime exemption. Evaluate the gift tax consequences of Margaret's exercise of the power.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Margaret's exercise is treated as a $2,962,000 taxable gift ($3,000,000 less two annual exclusions of $19,000 each) that further reduces her lifetime exemption", "No gift tax consequences arise because Margaret is exercising a power created by her father, not transferring her own property", "The exercise is treated as a distribution from the father's trust and is taxable to the trust, not to Margaret", "Margaret owes gift tax only on the amount exceeding the trust's original funding value"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 2514(b), the exercise of a general power of appointment is treated as a transfer by the power holder for gift tax purposes. Margaret is treated as if she owned the trust assets and gifted them. The gift is $3,000,000 less two annual exclusions ($19,000 each) = $2,962,000, reducing her remaining lifetime exemption. Wrong (B): Section 2514 treats the power holder as the transferor regardless of who created the trust. Wrong (C): The trust is not the taxpayer for gift tax purposes when a power holder exercises a general power. Wrong (D): The original funding value of the trust is irrelevant to the gift tax computation on exercise.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Sec 2036(a)(2) retained right to designate enjoyment
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Beatrice Caldwell transferred $4,000,000 in securities to an irrevocable trust for her three children in 2018. The transfer was a completed gift for gift tax purposes. However, the trust agreement gives Beatrice the sole discretion to determine how income and principal are distributed among the three children. Beatrice dies in 2026 when the trust assets are worth $6,500,000. Beatrice's estate planning attorney argues the assets were removed from the estate by the completed gift. Assess the estate tax treatment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The trust assets are excluded from Beatrice's estate because the gift was completed and irrevocable", "The full $6,500,000 is included in Beatrice's gross estate under Section 2036(a)(2) because she retained the right to designate who enjoys the trust property", "Only the $4,000,000 original transfer is included because estate inclusion is limited to the gift-date value", "Only the income interest is included; the remainder interest passes free of estate tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 2036(a)(2) includes transferred property in the gross estate when the decedent retained the right to designate the persons who shall possess or enjoy the property or its income. Beatrice's sole discretion over distributions among the children is exactly this retained power. The full date-of-death value of $6,500,000 is included. Wrong (A): A completed gift for gift tax purposes does not prevent estate inclusion when the transferor retains powers described in Section 2036. Wrong (C): Estate inclusion uses the date-of-death FMV, not the original transfer value. Wrong (D): Section 2036(a)(2) includes the entire transferred property, not just the income interest.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Sec 2703 buy-sell agreement disregarded for valuation
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Victor Hargrove and his two children are partners in a family business. They execute a buy-sell agreement that restricts any partner from selling their interest for more than $500,000. Victor dies in 2026 when the fair market value of his interest, determined without regard to the restriction, is $2,000,000. The agreement was entered into primarily as a device to transfer the interest to Victor's children for less than adequate consideration. Assess how Section 2703 affects the estate tax valuation.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The agreement price of $500,000 controls because buy-sell agreements are binding on the IRS", "The IRS must accept the $500,000 price if the agreement was commercially reasonable at inception", "The restriction is disregarded and the interest is valued at $2,000,000 because the agreement does not satisfy the Section 2703(b) safe harbor requirements", "The value is the average of $500,000 and $2,000,000 because Section 2703 requires a blended approach"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 2703(a) provides that the value of property for estate and gift tax purposes is determined without regard to any option, agreement, or restriction on the right to sell or use the property. The restriction is disregarded unless the agreement satisfies all three requirements of Section 2703(b): it is a bona fide business arrangement, it is not a device to transfer property for less than adequate consideration, and its terms are comparable to similar arm's-length arrangements. Here, the device purpose disqualifies the agreement. Wrong (A): Buy-sell agreements are not automatically binding on the IRS for valuation purposes. Wrong (B): Commercial reasonableness alone is insufficient; all three Section 2703(b) requirements must be met. Wrong (D): There is no blended valuation approach under Section 2703.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Adequate and full consideration exception
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$Under federal gift tax law, what is the effect of adequate and full consideration on a transfer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It converts the transfer from a gift to a bequest", "It increases the donor's lifetime exemption by the amount of consideration received", "It requires the donor to file Form 709 to report the consideration", "It prevents the transfer from being treated as a gift because no gratuitous transfer has occurred"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 2512(b), a transfer for adequate and full consideration in money or money's worth is not a gift. The gift tax applies only to the extent that the FMV of the transferred property exceeds the consideration received. A bona fide arm's-length sale eliminates the gift element entirely. Wrong (A): Consideration does not convert a lifetime transfer into a bequest; bequests occur only at death. Wrong (B): Receiving consideration does not increase the lifetime exemption amount. Wrong (C): A transfer for full consideration is not a reportable gift and does not require Form 709.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Portability NOT available for GST exemption
(6, 'Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies', $EXPL$James Whitfield dies in 2026, having used none of his $13,990,000 GST exemption. His executor files Form 706 and properly elects portability of James's unused estate tax exemption to his surviving wife, Carol. Carol's estate planning attorney advises her on the available exemptions. Assess whether Carol can use James's unused GST exemption for her own generation-skipping transfers.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Carol cannot use James's GST exemption because portability under Section 2010(c)(4) applies only to the estate and gift tax exemption, not the GST exemption", "Carol can use the full $13,990,000 GST exemption because the portability election covers all transfer tax exemptions", "Carol can use half of James's unused GST exemption under a partial portability rule", "Carol can use James's GST exemption only for direct skips, not for transfers to trusts"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Portability under Section 2010(c)(4) allows the surviving spouse to use the deceased spouse's unused exclusion amount (DSUE) only for estate and gift tax purposes. The GST exemption under Section 2631 is not portable. Carol is limited to her own GST exemption for generation-skipping transfers. Wrong (B): The portability election does not extend to the GST exemption; this is a critical limitation. Wrong (C): There is no partial portability rule for GST exemption. Wrong (D): The non-portability of GST exemption applies to all types of generation-skipping transfers, not just trust transfers.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

